('Ordinary Biology','Form 1','Cell membrane','cell-membrane-structure-and-transport','The Cell Membrane: Structure and Selective Transport','<h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li>Describe the fluid-mosaic model of the cell membrane.</li><li>Explain selective permeability.</li><li>Compare diffusion, osmosis and active transport.</li></ul><h2>Membrane structure</h2><p>The cell membrane forms the boundary between the cell and its surroundings. It consists mainly of a phospholipid bilayer with proteins, cholesterol in many animal cells, and carbohydrate groups attached to some lipids and proteins. Each phospholipid has a hydrophilic phosphate-containing head and hydrophobic fatty-acid tails. In water, the heads face the watery environments while the tails point inward, forming a flexible bilayer.</p><p>Membrane proteins have different functions. Channel and carrier proteins help selected substances cross the bilayer; receptors detect chemical signals; enzymes catalyse reactions; and some proteins help cells attach to one another or recognise other cells. Cholesterol helps regulate membrane fluidity in animal cells. The fluid-mosaic model describes a dynamic membrane in which many components can move laterally.</p><h2>Selective permeability</h2><p>The membrane does not allow every substance to pass at the same rate. Small non-polar molecules can often diffuse through the lipid bilayer, whereas ions and many polar molecules need membrane proteins. The permeability of a substance depends on size, charge, lipid solubility, concentration gradients and the proteins present.</p><h2>Transport across membranes</h2><p>Diffusion is net movement down a concentration gradient and does not require cellular energy. Osmosis is the net movement of water across a selectively permeable membrane from a region of higher water potential to lower water potential. Facilitated diffusion uses a channel or carrier protein but still moves down a gradient. Active transport moves a substance against its electrochemical gradient and requires energy, commonly from ATP or a coupled ion gradient.</p><h2>Applying the concepts</h2><p>When a plant cell is placed in a dilute solution, water may enter by osmosis and increase turgor pressure. In a concentrated solution, water may leave and the cell membrane can pull away from the cell wall during plasmolysis. An animal cell placed in a very dilute solution may swell because it has no rigid cell wall.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>The phospholipid bilayer creates a flexible, selectively permeable barrier.</li><li>Membrane proteins support transport, signalling and recognition.</li><li>Diffusion and facilitated diffusion move down gradients; active transport can move against a gradient using energy.</li></ul>','Explore the phospholipid bilayer, membrane proteins, selective permeability and transport mechanisms.','AliverBiopharm','Cell Biology','cell-membrane','lesson','4 min read',460,1,false,true,'{"group":"Form 1","level":"O Level","key_terms":["phospholipid bilayer","fluid-mosaic model","osmosis","facilitated diffusion","active transport"],"curriculum_aligned":true,"learning_objectives":["Describe the membrane","Explain selective permeability","Compare membrane transport"]}'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb),
('Ordinary Biology','Form 1','The Five Kingdoms','five-kingdom-classification-and-characteristics','The Five Kingdoms: Comparing Major Groups of Living Things','<h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li>Describe the main features used in biological classification.</li><li>Compare the traditional five kingdoms.</li><li>Recognise the limits of a simple kingdom system.</li></ul><h2>Why group organisms?</h2><p>Classification helps biologists organise biodiversity, identify organisms and communicate observations consistently. Useful features include cell type, number of cells, cell-wall composition, mode of nutrition, reproduction and evidence of evolutionary relationships. A classification system is a scientific model and can change as new evidence becomes available.</p><h2>The traditional five kingdoms</h2><ul><li><strong>Monera:</strong> in the traditional school system, this group includes prokaryotes such as bacteria. Their cells lack a membrane-bound nucleus. Many have cell walls, and they may obtain nutrients in different ways.</li><li><strong>Protoctista:</strong> a diverse collection of mostly simple eukaryotes, including many unicellular organisms such as Amoeba and Euglena, as well as some algae. Their structures and nutrition vary widely.</li><li><strong>Fungi:</strong> eukaryotes that absorb nutrients from organic material. Their cell walls usually contain chitin; many grow as hyphae forming a mycelium, while yeasts are unicellular. Fungi commonly reproduce by spores.</li><li><strong>Plantae:</strong> multicellular eukaryotes with cellulose cell walls. Most carry out photosynthesis using chlorophyll in chloroplasts and store carbohydrate mainly as starch.</li><li><strong>Animalia:</strong> multicellular eukaryotes without cell walls. Animals obtain nutrients by ingestion and typically show specialised tissues and active responses to stimuli.</li></ul><h2>Compare carefully</h2><p>Do not classify an organism using one feature alone. For example, not every organism that is green belongs to Plantae, and fungi are not plants because they absorb nutrients rather than photosynthesise. Bacteria are prokaryotic, whereas organisms in the other four traditional kingdoms are eukaryotic.</p><h2>Modern classification</h2><p>Modern systems often separate prokaryotes into Bacteria and Archaea and use molecular evidence to investigate ancestry. The traditional five-kingdom system remains useful for introductory comparisons, but it does not represent every detail of evolutionary relationships.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>Cell type, cell structure and nutrition are important classification features.</li><li>Fungi absorb nutrients; plants commonly photosynthesise; animals ingest food.</li><li>Classification systems are refined as evidence improves.</li></ul>','Compare the five traditional kingdoms using cell structure, nutrition, cell walls and modern classification evidence.','AliverBiopharm','Classification','five-kingdoms','lesson','4 min read',450,1,false,true,'{"group":"Form 1","level":"O Level","key_terms":["prokaryote","eukaryote","Monera","Protoctista","Fungi","Plantae","Animalia"],"curriculum_aligned":true,"learning_objectives":["Compare kingdom characteristics","Distinguish prokaryotes from eukaryotes","Explain limits of classification"]}'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb),
('Ordinary Biology','Form 2','Factors Affecting Photosynthesis','factors-affecting-photosynthesis-limiting-factors','Factors Affecting Photosynthesis and Limiting Factors','<h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li>Identify major factors that affect photosynthetic rate.</li><li>Explain the idea of a limiting factor.</li><li>Plan a fair investigation of photosynthesis.</li></ul><h2>Photosynthetic rate</h2><p>Photosynthesis uses light energy to build organic molecules from carbon dioxide and water. Its rate depends on the availability of light, carbon dioxide and water, as well as temperature and the condition of the photosynthetic tissues. The factor in shortest effective supply can limit the overall rate, even when other factors are plentiful.</p><h2>Light intensity</h2><p>When light is limiting, increasing light intensity usually increases the rate because more photons can be absorbed by photosynthetic pigments. The increase eventually levels off when another factor becomes limiting. Very intense light can cause damage under some conditions, so the rate does not rise without limit.</p><h2>Carbon dioxide concentration</h2><p>Carbon dioxide supplies the carbon used in carbohydrate formation. Raising its concentration can increase photosynthetic rate while carbon dioxide is limiting. Beyond a suitable range, the rate plateaus because light, temperature or another factor limits the reactions.</p><h2>Temperature and water</h2><p>Photosynthesis includes enzyme-controlled reactions. A moderate temperature rise can increase reaction rates towards an optimum; excessive heat can disrupt enzymes and membranes. Water is a reactant, and water shortage may also cause stomata to close, reducing carbon dioxide entry. Plants can therefore show reduced photosynthesis during drought even in bright sunlight.</p><h2>Investigating the effect of light</h2><p>A common practical uses pondweed and counts oxygen bubbles or measures collected oxygen over a fixed time. Bubble size can vary, so collected gas volume is usually a better measure where equipment permits. Change one independent variable at a time and keep temperature, carbon dioxide availability, plant size and measurement interval constant. Repeat trials and calculate a mean. If moving a lamp closer increases both light and temperature, temperature must be controlled or monitored.</p><h2>Interpreting graphs</h2><p>A curve that rises and then plateaus indicates that the changed factor was initially limiting but another factor became limiting. When comparing curves at different carbon dioxide concentrations, a higher plateau may indicate that more carbon dioxide allows a greater rate under those conditions.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>Light, carbon dioxide, water and temperature all affect photosynthesis.</li><li>A limiting factor constrains the rate when other factors cannot compensate.</li><li>Fair tests control other variables and use repeatable measurements.</li></ul>','Understand light intensity, carbon dioxide, temperature, water and the design of photosynthesis investigations.','AliverBiopharm','Plant Nutrition','photosynthesis-limiting-factors','lesson','4 min read',470,1,false,true,'{"group":"Form 2","level":"O Level","key_terms":["limiting factor","light intensity","carbon dioxide","stomata","photosynthetic rate"],"curriculum_aligned":true,"learning_objectives":["Explain limiting factors","Interpret photosynthesis graphs","Plan a fair practical"]}'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb),
('Ordinary Biology','Form 2','Digestive Enzymes','digestive-enzymes-substrates-and-products','Digestive Enzymes: Substrates, Products and Conditions','<h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li>Explain why large food molecules must be digested.</li><li>Match major digestive enzymes with substrates and products.</li><li>Relate enzyme activity to pH and temperature.</li></ul><h2>Why digestion is necessary</h2><p>Many food molecules are too large or chemically unsuitable to pass through the gut wall directly. Mechanical digestion breaks food into smaller pieces and increases surface area. Chemical digestion uses enzymes to hydrolyse large molecules into smaller soluble products that can be absorbed.</p><h2>Carbohydrate digestion</h2><p>Amylase breaks starch into smaller carbohydrates, including maltose and short chains. Maltase at the small-intestinal surface converts maltose into glucose. Glucose is absorbed into the blood and can be used in respiration or stored after conversion to glycogen. Amylase does not digest cellulose because humans lack cellulase.</p><h2>Protein digestion</h2><p>Proteases hydrolyse peptide bonds in proteins. Pepsin acts in the acidic environment of the stomach, while pancreatic proteases act in the small intestine. Peptides are further broken down by enzymes such as peptidases to amino acids, which are absorbed and used for growth, repair and synthesis of proteins and other nitrogen-containing compounds.</p><h2>Fat digestion</h2><p>Lipase breaks triglycerides into fatty acids and monoglycerides or glycerol-related products, depending on the enzyme and conditions. Bile is not an enzyme: it emulsifies large fat droplets into smaller droplets, increasing the surface area available to lipase. Most fat digestion and absorption occurs in the small intestine.</p><h2>Conditions and specificity</h2><p>Each enzyme has a suitable temperature and pH range. Extreme heat can disrupt the structure of protein enzymes; an unsuitable pH can alter charges and the shape of the active site. Enzymes are substrate-specific because their active sites have complementary chemical properties. They increase reaction rate but are not used up in the reaction.</p><h2>Applying the knowledge</h2><p>When testing amylase, starch disappearance can be monitored using iodine solution. Temperature, pH, enzyme concentration, starch concentration and sampling intervals should be controlled. Use a suitable control and repeat measurements. Iodine tests for starch, not directly for glucose.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>Amylase digests starch, proteases digest proteins, and lipases digest fats.</li><li>Bile helps fat digestion by emulsification but is not an enzyme.</li><li>Enzyme activity depends on substrate, temperature, pH and other conditions.</li></ul>','Review amylase, proteases and lipases, their substrates and products, and the conditions needed for digestion.','AliverBiopharm','Animal Nutrition','digestive-enzymes','lesson','4 min read',470,1,false,true,'{"group":"Form 2","level":"O Level","key_terms":["amylase","protease","lipase","hydrolysis","emulsification"],"curriculum_aligned":true,"learning_objectives":["Match digestive enzymes to substrates","Explain bile and emulsification","Describe enzyme conditions"]}'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb),
('Ordinary Biology','Form 3','Reflex Actions','reflex-arc-and-nervous-coordination','Reflex Actions: The Reflex Arc and Rapid Responses','<h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li>Describe the pathway of a reflex arc.</li><li>Explain why reflex actions can be rapid and protective.</li><li>Distinguish a reflex from a voluntary response.</li></ul><h2>What is a reflex?</h2><p>A reflex action is a rapid, automatic response to a stimulus. Many reflexes protect the body from harm, such as withdrawing a hand from a hot object or blinking when something approaches the eye. Reflexes can occur without a deliberate decision, although the brain can become aware of the stimulus and influence some responses.</p><h2>The reflex arc</h2><p>A typical spinal reflex arc involves a receptor, sensory neurone, relay neurone in the central nervous system, motor neurone and effector. The receptor detects a stimulus and converts it into an electrical signal. The sensory neurone carries impulses towards the spinal cord, where synapses transmit the signal to relay and motor neurones. The motor neurone carries the impulse to an effector such as a muscle or gland, which produces the response.</p><h2>Synapses</h2><p>At a chemical synapse, an arriving electrical impulse triggers release of neurotransmitter from a neurone. The neurotransmitter diffuses across the synaptic cleft and binds to receptors on the next cell, changing its activity. Synaptic transmission has a short delay and usually travels in one direction. The pathway is organised to produce an appropriate response quickly.</p><h2>Why reflexes are useful</h2><p>Automatic responses reduce the delay associated with conscious decision-making and can limit injury. For example, the withdrawal reflex activates muscles that move a limb away from a painful stimulus. Pain perception involves additional pathways to the brain; the withdrawal can begin before a person has fully processed the sensation consciously.</p><h2>Voluntary and reflex actions</h2><p>Voluntary actions are shaped by conscious planning and brain activity. Reflex actions are stereotyped and automatic, although they are still coordinated by the nervous system. Not every reflex is controlled by the spinal cord; some, including pupil responses, involve brainstem circuits.</p><h2>Exam method</h2><p>When describing a reflex arc, give the sequence in order and state the role of each part. Do not skip the receptor or effector. Distinguish the direction of impulses in sensory and motor neurones, and explain how the response helps protect or regulate the body.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>A reflex arc links receptor, sensory pathway, central processing, motor pathway and effector.</li><li>Synapses pass signals between cells using neurotransmitters in many pathways.</li><li>Reflexes are automatic responses that often help protect the body.</li></ul>','Learn the reflex arc, synaptic transmission and how rapid automatic responses protect the body.','AliverBiopharm','Coordination and Response','reflex-actions','lesson','4 min read',450,1,false,true,'{"group":"Form 3","level":"O Level","key_terms":["receptor","sensory neurone","relay neurone","motor neurone","effector","synapse"],"curriculum_aligned":true,"learning_objectives":["Describe a reflex arc","Explain synapses","Compare reflex and voluntary actions"]}'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb),
('Ordinary Biology','Form 4','Ecosystems','ecosystems-food-webs-and-energy-flow','Ecosystems: Food Webs, Energy Flow and Population Balance','<h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li>Define ecosystem, population, community and habitat.</li><li>Interpret food chains and food webs.</li><li>Explain energy transfer and the role of decomposers.</li></ul><h2>Ecological organisation</h2><p>An ecosystem includes a community of organisms and the non-living environment with which they interact. A population is a group of organisms of the same species in an area; a community includes the populations of different species; and a habitat is the place where an organism lives. Abiotic factors such as light, water, temperature, pH and mineral availability influence which organisms can survive.</p><h2>Feeding relationships</h2><p>Producers, commonly green plants and algae, capture energy and make organic molecules. Primary consumers feed on producers, secondary consumers feed on primary consumers, and higher consumers may feed at several trophic levels. A food chain shows one route of energy transfer, while a food web connects many feeding relationships in a community. Arrows point from the food organism to the organism that obtains energy by eating it.</p><h2>Energy transfer</h2><p>Energy is lost between trophic levels through respiration, movement, heat, waste and uneaten material. Consequently, less energy is usually available to support biomass at higher trophic levels. Energy flows through an ecosystem rather than being recycled. Matter, including carbon and mineral nutrients, is recycled through biological and physical processes.</p><h2>Decomposers and nutrient cycling</h2><p>Bacteria and fungi break down dead organisms and waste. Decomposition releases mineral nutrients that producers can absorb again. The rate of decomposition depends on temperature, moisture, oxygen availability and the chemical composition of the material. Without decomposers, nutrients would remain locked in dead organic matter for longer.</p><h2>Population changes</h2><p>Population size can change through births, deaths, immigration and emigration. Food supply, competition, predation, disease and abiotic conditions affect survival and reproduction. A change in one population can affect other populations in the food web, so ecosystem relationships are interconnected.</p><h2>Human impact and conservation</h2><p>Habitat destruction, pollution, overharvesting and invasive species can reduce biodiversity. Conservation can include habitat protection, sustainable resource use, restoration and monitoring. A sound conclusion should be based on evidence and consider ecological as well as social needs.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>Food webs show interconnected feeding relationships.</li><li>Energy flows and decreases between trophic levels; nutrients can be recycled.</li><li>Changes in one population can affect the wider ecosystem.</li></ul>','Understand ecosystem terminology, food webs, energy transfer, nutrient cycling and population change.','AliverBiopharm','Ecology','ecosystems-food-webs','lesson','4 min read',470,1,false,true,'{"group":"Form 4","level":"O Level","key_terms":["ecosystem","population","community","trophic level","decomposer","biodiversity"],"curriculum_aligned":true,"learning_objectives":["Define ecological terms","Interpret food webs","Explain energy transfer and decomposition"]}'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb),
('Advanced Biology','Form 5','Membrane Transport','membrane-transport-diffusion-osmosis-active-transport','Membrane Transport: Diffusion, Osmosis and Active Transport','<h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li>Compare simple and facilitated diffusion.</li><li>Explain water potential and osmosis.</li><li>Relate active transport to ATP and electrochemical gradients.</li></ul><h2>Membranes and gradients</h2><p>Biological membranes are selectively permeable. The movement of substances depends on concentration differences, electrical charge, membrane permeability and transport proteins. For ions, the combined effect of chemical and electrical gradients is called the electrochemical gradient. Cells maintain these gradients to support nutrient uptake, nerve signalling and many other functions.</p><h2>Simple diffusion</h2><p>Diffusion is the net movement of particles from higher to lower concentration as a result of random molecular motion. The rate is influenced by the size of the concentration gradient, temperature, surface area, diffusion distance and properties of the substance. Small non-polar molecules can cross the lipid bilayer more readily than charged particles.</p><h2>Facilitated diffusion</h2><p>Channel proteins provide selective pathways through the membrane, while carrier proteins bind a substance and change shape to move it across. Facilitated diffusion moves substances down their electrochemical gradient and does not directly require ATP. Because the number of transport proteins is limited, the rate can saturate when available carriers are occupied.</p><h2>Osmosis and water potential</h2><p>Osmosis is net water movement across a selectively permeable membrane from higher water potential to lower water potential. Solute concentration affects water potential, and pressure can also contribute. Plant cells in dilute surroundings tend to gain water and become turgid; in concentrated surroundings they can lose water and become plasmolysed. Animal cells lack a cell wall, so large water movements can cause swelling or shrinkage.</p><h2>Active transport</h2><p>Active transport moves substances against a gradient using energy. Primary active transport uses energy directly, often through ATP hydrolysis; secondary active transport couples movement of one substance down its gradient to movement of another against its gradient. The sodium-potassium pump is an example of primary active transport that helps maintain ion gradients across animal cell membranes.</p><h2>Experimental application</h2><p>When investigating osmosis in plant tissue, use equal-sized samples, measure initial and final mass, keep temperature and time constant, and test a range of solution concentrations. Percentage mass change is calculated as (final mass − initial mass) ÷ initial mass × 100. A graph can help estimate the external concentration at which there is no net mass change.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>Diffusion and facilitated diffusion move down gradients without direct ATP use.</li><li>Osmosis is water movement down a water-potential gradient.</li><li>Active transport requires an energy source to move substances against a gradient.</li></ul>','Compare transport mechanisms, water potential, carrier saturation and the role of ATP in membrane transport.','AliverBiopharm','Membrane Transport','membrane-transport','lesson','5 min read',520,1,false,true,'{"group":"Form 5","level":"A Level","key_terms":["electrochemical gradient","facilitated diffusion","water potential","osmosis","ATP","carrier protein"],"curriculum_aligned":true,"learning_objectives":["Compare diffusion mechanisms","Explain water potential","Describe active transport"]}'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb),
('Advanced Biology','Form 6','Gene Expression','gene-expression-transcription-translation','Gene Expression: From DNA to Functional Protein','<h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li>Explain transcription and translation.</li><li>Describe the roles of mRNA, tRNA and ribosomes.</li><li>Relate mutations to possible changes in protein function.</li></ul><h2>From gene to product</h2><p>Gene expression is the use of information in DNA to produce a functional product, often a protein. In eukaryotic cells, transcription occurs in the nucleus and translation occurs on ribosomes in the cytoplasm or on the rough endoplasmic reticulum. The sequence of bases in DNA determines the sequence of bases in messenger RNA, which is interpreted in groups of three called codons.</p><h2>Transcription</h2><p>RNA polymerase binds to regulatory DNA near a gene and synthesises RNA using one DNA strand as a template. RNA nucleotides pair with the template according to base-pairing rules, with uracil replacing thymine in RNA. In eukaryotes, the initial transcript is processed: a cap and poly-A tail are added, and introns are removed while exons are joined by splicing. Alternative splicing can allow one gene to produce different RNA transcripts.</p><h2>Translation</h2><p>A ribosome reads mRNA codons. Transfer RNA molecules carry amino acids and have anticodons that pair with complementary codons. The ribosome catalyses peptide-bond formation as amino acids are joined in the order specified by the mRNA. Translation usually begins at a start codon and ends when a stop codon is reached. The resulting polypeptide folds and may undergo further processing before becoming functional.</p><h2>Genetic code</h2><p>The genetic code is read in non-overlapping triplets, is degenerate because several codons can specify the same amino acid, and is nearly universal across life. A base substitution may be silent, missense or nonsense depending on its effect on the codon. Insertions or deletions not divisible by three can cause a frameshift, changing many downstream codons. The effect of a mutation depends on its location, the protein and the biological context.</p><h2>Regulation and environment</h2><p>Cells regulate gene expression through transcription factors, chromatin structure, RNA processing, RNA stability and translation. Different cell types can contain essentially the same genome but express different sets of genes. Environmental signals can alter expression, allowing cells to adapt their activity.</p><h2>Exam approach</h2><p>Use the correct template and coding strands when deriving RNA sequences, state whether the sequence is written 5′ to 3′, and identify the reading frame before translating codons. Do not assume every DNA mutation changes the amino-acid sequence or that every amino-acid change causes a detectable phenotype.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>Transcription copies information from DNA into RNA.</li><li>Translation uses mRNA codons to assemble an amino-acid sequence.</li><li>Mutations can have different effects, and gene expression is regulated at several stages.</li></ul>','Follow transcription, RNA processing and translation, then connect mutations with possible protein effects.','AliverBiopharm','Gene Expression','gene-expression','lesson','5 min read',520,1,false,true,'{"group":"Form 6","level":"A Level","key_terms":["RNA polymerase","mRNA","tRNA","codon","splicing","frameshift"],"curriculum_aligned":true,"learning_objectives":["Explain transcription and translation","Describe RNA processing","Interpret mutation effects"]}'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb),
('Pharmacy','Certificate','Pharmacokinetics','pharmacokinetics-adme-and-drug-exposure','Pharmacokinetics: Absorption, Distribution, Metabolism and Excretion','<h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li>Define the four ADME processes.</li><li>Explain how route and formulation affect drug exposure.</li><li>Recognise the role of clearance and half-life in dosing.</li></ul><h2>What pharmacokinetics studies</h2><p>Pharmacokinetics describes how the body handles a medicine over time. The four broad processes are absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion (ADME). Together they influence the concentration of drug and active metabolites reaching the site of action, the duration of effect and the potential for accumulation.</p><h2>Absorption and bioavailability</h2><p>Absorption is movement of a medicine from its administration site into systemic circulation. It depends on formulation, dissolution, route, blood flow, membrane permeability, gastrointestinal conditions and interactions with food or other medicines. Bioavailability is the fraction of an administered dose that reaches systemic circulation unchanged. Intravenous administration has 100% systemic bioavailability by definition, whereas oral medicines may undergo incomplete absorption and first-pass metabolism.</p><h2>Distribution</h2><p>Distribution is reversible movement between blood and tissues. It is affected by tissue perfusion, lipid solubility, ionisation, plasma-protein binding and biological barriers. The apparent volume of distribution relates the amount of drug in the body to its measured plasma concentration; it is a proportionality measure, not necessarily a real anatomical volume.</p><h2>Metabolism</h2><p>Metabolism chemically transforms a drug, often through enzymes in the liver but also in other tissues. Some metabolites are inactive, some retain activity, and a few medicines are administered as prodrugs that require conversion to an active form. Enzyme inhibition can increase exposure to a substrate drug, while enzyme induction can reduce exposure, although the clinical effect depends on the particular pathways and medicines.</p><h2>Excretion, clearance and half-life</h2><p>Excretion removes a drug or its metabolites, commonly through the kidneys or bile. Clearance describes the volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit time. Half-life is the time for plasma concentration to fall by half during a defined elimination phase. For many drugs with approximately linear kinetics, half-life depends on both clearance and apparent distribution volume. Reduced kidney or liver function can change exposure and may require clinician-led adjustment.</p><h2>Clinical application and safety</h2><p>Age, organ function, pregnancy, genetics, adherence and interacting medicines can affect pharmacokinetics. A loading dose may be used when a target concentration is needed quickly, while maintenance dosing aims to replace drug eliminated over time. These concepts guide authorised prescribing, but patient-specific decisions must follow current guidelines and product information.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>ADME describes absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion.</li><li>Bioavailability, clearance, distribution volume and half-life describe different aspects of exposure.</li><li>Clinical dosing decisions require individual assessment and authoritative guidance.</li></ul><p><em>Educational material only; not an individual dosing recommendation.</em></p>','Understand ADME, bioavailability, distribution volume, clearance and half-life in drug exposure.','AliverBiopharm','Basic Pharmacology','pharmacokinetics','lesson','5 min read',540,1,false,true,'{"group":"Certificate","level":"Pharmacy","key_terms":["ADME","bioavailability","volume of distribution","clearance","half-life"],"clinical_note":true,"curriculum_aligned":true,"learning_objectives":["Explain ADME","Define bioavailability and clearance","Relate half-life to drug exposure"]}'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb),
('Pharmacy','Certificate','Drug Interactions','drug-interactions-pharmacokinetic-and-pharmacodynamic','Drug Interactions: Mechanisms, Risk and Prevention','<h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li>Distinguish pharmacokinetic from pharmacodynamic interactions.</li><li>Identify patient and medicine factors that increase interaction risk.</li><li>Describe safe steps for recognising and managing a possible interaction.</li></ul><h2>What is a drug interaction?</h2><p>A drug interaction occurs when another medicine, food, supplement, alcohol or a disease state changes the effect or exposure of a medicine. Interactions may increase toxicity, reduce therapeutic effect or occasionally be used deliberately. The significance depends on the medicines, dose, patient characteristics and clinical context; not every listed interaction causes harm in every person.</p><h2>Pharmacokinetic interactions</h2><p>These change absorption, distribution, metabolism or excretion. A medicine can alter stomach acidity or bind another product and reduce absorption. Enzyme inhibition can increase the concentration of a drug metabolised by that enzyme; enzyme induction can lower concentrations by increasing metabolism. Changes in kidney function or competition for renal transport can also alter exposure. The direction and size of an interaction depend on the specific pathway.</p><h2>Pharmacodynamic interactions</h2><p>These occur when effects combine at physiological targets or systems, even when drug concentrations are unchanged. Two sedating medicines may increase drowsiness; medicines that lower blood pressure may produce additive hypotension; and medicines that affect bleeding may increase bleeding risk when combined. Some combinations oppose one another and reduce benefit.</p><h2>Risk assessment</h2><p>Risk is influenced by narrow therapeutic windows, high doses, multiple medicines, frailty, kidney or liver impairment and a history of adverse reactions. Prescription medicines, over-the-counter products, herbal products and supplements all belong in a complete medication history. A change to one medicine can alter the safety of the entire regimen.</p><h2>Practical prevention</h2><ul><li>Reconcile the complete medication list, including non-prescription products and allergies.</li><li>Check a current, trusted interaction resource and the relevant product information.</li><li>Consider the seriousness, evidence, dose, timing and patient-specific risk.</li><li>Discuss alternatives, monitoring or dose changes with the authorised prescriber or pharmacist when indicated.</li><li>Explain warning symptoms and when urgent assessment is needed.</li></ul><h2>Reporting and counselling</h2><p>Document suspected interactions and the actions taken. Do not tell a patient to stop a prescribed medicine abruptly without appropriate clinical advice. Serious symptoms such as collapse, breathing difficulty, severe bleeding or signs of a severe allergic reaction require urgent medical assessment.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>Pharmacokinetic interactions change exposure; pharmacodynamic interactions change combined effects.</li><li>Interaction databases support, but do not replace, clinical judgement.</li><li>Medication reconciliation and clear communication reduce preventable harm.</li></ul><p><em>Educational material only. Check current local guidance and seek professional assessment for real cases.</em></p>','Learn interaction mechanisms, risk factors, medication reconciliation and safe counselling principles.','AliverBiopharm','Basic Pharmacology','drug-interactions','lesson','4 min read',500,1,false,true,'{"group":"Certificate","level":"Pharmacy","key_terms":["pharmacokinetic interaction","pharmacodynamic interaction","enzyme inhibition","enzyme induction","medication reconciliation"],"clinical_note":true,"curriculum_aligned":true,"learning_objectives":["Compare interaction mechanisms","Recognise risk factors","Describe prevention and counselling"]}'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb),
('Pharmacy','Diploma','Concentration and Dilution','pharmaceutical-concentration-and-dilution-calculations','Concentration and Dilution: A Step-by-Step Method','<h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li>Calculate mass-per-volume concentrations.</li><li>Use the dilution equation when the amount of solute remains constant.</li><li>Check units and identify when an additional clinical check is required.</li></ul><h2>Expressing concentration</h2><p>Concentration describes the amount of solute in a stated amount of solution. A mass-per-volume concentration may be expressed as mg/mL or g/L. For example, if 500 mg of a substance is present in a final volume of 10 mL, the concentration is 500 mg ÷ 10 mL = 50 mg/mL. Always use the final solution volume, not simply the volume of solvent added.</p><h2>Metric conversions</h2><ul><li>1 g = 1,000 mg.</li><li>1 mg = 1,000 micrograms.</li><li>1 L = 1,000 mL.</li></ul><p>Convert values into compatible units before calculating. Keep the unit beside each number so that incorrect cancellation is visible. Do not assume that a percentage is mass/volume unless the formulation specifies that basis.</p><h2>Dilution equation</h2><p>When a dilution is made by adding solvent and no solute is lost, the amount of solute before and after dilution is the same. This gives C₁V₁ = C₂V₂, where C₁ and V₁ are the starting concentration and volume, and C₂ and V₂ are the final concentration and volume. Use compatible concentration units and volume units on both sides.</p><p>Educational example: to prepare 100 mL of a 10 mg/mL solution from a 50 mg/mL stock, V₁ = (C₂ × V₂) ÷ C₁ = (10 mg/mL × 100 mL) ÷ 50 mg/mL = 20 mL of stock. The final preparation would be brought to a total volume of 100 mL using an appropriate diluent. The arithmetic alone does not establish that the proposed formulation or diluent is suitable.</p><h2>Common errors</h2><ul><li>Confusing the volume of stock with the final volume.</li><li>Mixing mg with g or mL with L without conversion.</li><li>Rounding too early or omitting units.</li><li>Applying the dilution equation when solute is lost, reacts or changes form.</li></ul><h2>Professional safety</h2><p>Actual preparation requires verification of the authorised formula, product compatibility, sterility requirements, stability, container, labelling and local compounding procedures. High-risk preparations should receive the independent checks required by policy. Ask a qualified supervisor if a calculation, unit or instruction is unclear.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>Concentration is amount of solute divided by final solution volume.</li><li>C₁V₁ = C₂V₂ applies to a simple dilution with conserved solute.</li><li>Correct arithmetic must be paired with formulation and clinical verification.</li></ul><p><em>For learning only; not an instruction to prepare or administer a medicine.</em></p>','Practise concentration calculations and the dilution equation with explicit units and safety checks.','AliverBiopharm','Pharmaceutical Calculations','concentration-and-dilution','lesson','4 min read',490,1,false,true,'{"group":"Diploma","level":"Pharmacy","key_terms":["concentration","dilution","stock solution","final volume","dimensional analysis"],"clinical_note":true,"curriculum_aligned":true,"learning_objectives":["Calculate concentration","Apply C1V1=C2V2","Recognise preparation safety checks"]}'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb),
('Pharmacy','Degree','Structure-Activity Relationships','medicinal-chemistry-structure-activity-relationships','Structure–Activity Relationships in Medicinal Chemistry','<h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li>Define a structure–activity relationship (SAR).</li><li>Explain how molecular changes can alter potency, selectivity and exposure.</li><li>Recognise the limits of drawing conclusions from one structural feature.</li></ul><h2>What SAR means</h2><p>A structure–activity relationship links changes in a molecule to changes in a measured biological effect. Medicinal chemists compare related compounds, known as analogues, to understand which structural features support target binding, functional activity, selectivity, stability and pharmacokinetic behaviour. SAR conclusions are strongest when supported by a consistent set of experimental data rather than a single comparison.</p><h2>Interactions with a target</h2><p>Drug molecules may interact with targets through hydrogen bonds, ionic interactions, hydrophobic contacts, van der Waals forces and sometimes covalent bonds. Molecular shape and three-dimensional arrangement determine whether these interactions can occur in a binding site. A change in substituent position can alter geometry or steric fit even when the same functional group remains present.</p><h2>Potency, efficacy and selectivity</h2><p>Potency describes the concentration or dose needed to produce a specified effect, while efficacy describes the maximum effect under defined conditions. Selectivity describes preference for one target over others at relevant concentrations. Improving potency against a desired target does not automatically improve selectivity, clinical effectiveness or safety. Assay conditions and biological context must be considered when comparing results.</p><h2>Physicochemical and pharmacokinetic properties</h2><p>Changes to lipophilicity, ionisation, molecular size, hydrogen-bond donors and acceptors can alter solubility, membrane permeability, protein binding, metabolic stability and clearance. A more lipophilic analogue may cross a membrane more readily but may also have lower aqueous solubility or greater non-specific binding. Optimisation is therefore a balance across multiple properties, not a search for one maximum value.</p><h2>Common strategies</h2><ul><li>Modify a substituent to explore steric and electronic effects.</li><li>Change the position of a group to test binding geometry.</li><li>Replace a group with a bioisostere to preserve key interactions while changing stability or properties.</li><li>Restrict conformation to test whether a preferred shape improves binding.</li><li>Compare enantiomers when stereochemistry may affect target interactions or metabolism.</li></ul><h2>Interpreting SAR data</h2><p>Use appropriate controls, replicate measurements and comparable assay conditions. Distinguish correlation from causation, consider measurement uncertainty, and examine selectivity and toxicity alongside potency. A promising in-vitro result is a starting point for further testing, not proof of clinical benefit.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>SAR uses evidence to connect molecular structure with biological activity.</li><li>Binding, potency, efficacy, selectivity and exposure are distinct properties.</li><li>Drug optimisation balances target activity, safety and developability.</li></ul>','Connect molecular changes to target interactions, potency, selectivity and pharmacokinetic properties.','AliverBiopharm','Medicinal Chemistry','structure-activity-relationships','lesson','5 min read',520,1,false,true,'{"group":"Degree","level":"Pharmacy","key_terms":["analogue","bioisostere","selectivity","lipophilicity","stereochemistry","potency"],"clinical_note":true,"curriculum_aligned":true,"learning_objectives":["Define SAR","Explain molecular interactions","Balance activity and developability"]}'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb)
)
insert into public.notes
(unit_id,slug,title,content,content_preview,author,category,tag,section_type,read_time,word_count,display_order,is_premium,is_active,metadata,toc)
select u.id,s.slug,s.title,s.content,s.content_preview,s.author,s.category,s.tag,s.section_type,s.read_time,s.word_count,s.display_order,s.is_premium,s.is_active,s.metadata,s.toc
from seed s
join public.curriculum_levels l on l.display_name=s.level_name
join public.curriculum_groups g on g.level_id=l.id and g.name=s.group_name
join public.curriculum_units u on u.group_id=g.id and u.name=s.unit_name
on conflict (slug) do update set unit_id=excluded.unit_id,title=excluded.title,content=excluded.content,content_preview=excluded.content_preview,author=excluded.author,category=excluded.category,tag=excluded.tag,section_type=excluded.section_type,read_time=excluded.read_time,word_count=excluded.word_count,display_order=excluded.display_order,is_premium=excluded.is_premium,is_active=excluded.is_active,metadata=coalesce(public.notes.metadata,'{}'::jsonb)||excluded.metadata,toc=excluded.toc,updated_at=now();

insert into public.learning_content_links (curriculum_unit_id,content_type,content_id,relationship_type,is_primary,link_origin)
select n.unit_id,'note',n.id::text,'primary',true,'legacy_unit_id' from public.notes n where n.slug in ('cell-membrane-structure-and-transport','five-kingdom-classification-and-characteristics','factors-affecting-photosynthesis-limiting-factors','digestive-enzymes-substrates-and-products','reflex-arc-and-nervous-coordination','ecosystems-food-webs-and-energy-flow','membrane-transport-diffusion-osmosis-active-transport','gene-expression-transcription-translation','pharmacokinetics-adme-and-drug-exposure','drug-interactions-pharmacokinetic-and-pharmacodynamic','pharmaceutical-concentration-and-dilution-calculations','medicinal-chemistry-structure-activity-relationships')
on conflict (curriculum_unit_id,content_type,content_id,relationship_type) do update set is_primary=true,link_origin='legacy_unit_id',updated_at=now();
commit;